              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Curved walking pad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$680&ndash;$800</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rubber track over rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking, light jogging, under-desk use</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Entry slatted trainer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,095&ndash;$1,600</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slats, simpler resistance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking, jogging, occasional intervals</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Home sweet spot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,895&ndash;$2,800</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slats, 8 magnetic levels on most</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sprints, HIIT, sled-style pushes, regular running</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,995&ndash;$4,600</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slats, long belt or frame warranties</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heavy daily use, garage gyms, athletes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top end and club</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6,195&ndash;$15,250</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slats, bespoke surfaces and finishes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Buyers who want the best curve or the look</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Under $1,000: curved walking pads</h3>
          <p>
            The JOROTO, ERGOLIFE and Rongle machines on Amazon have a curved deck and no motor, but their surfaces are
            short and narrow: 41.34 by 15.75 inches on the JOROTO and 51.2 by 15.75 inches on the Rongle, per the
            listings. The JOROTO listing says plainly that it is for walking and light jogging, not running. These suit
            someone who wants a quiet, plug-free walking machine; they are not a cheap way into AssaultRunner-style
            sprint training.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">$1,095 to $1,600: entry slatted trainers</h3>
          <p>
            This is where real slat belts start. SB Fitness sells its CT250 walking model for $1,095 and its CT400 for
            $1,395 direct (the CT400 was $1,595 on Amazon the same day). Sunny&rsquo;s SF-X7140 was $1,337.23 on Amazon with
            magnetic resistance and a 330 lb rating. Expect fewer resistance settings, lighter frames and shorter
            warranties than the next tier.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">$1,895 to $2,800: the home sweet spot</h3>
          <p>
            Most home buyers end up here. The Sole C80 at $2,099.99 has a 19 by 62-inch slat deck, eight magnetic levels
            and a 330 lb rating on its Amazon listing; the SB Fitness CT550 ($1,895) and CT700 ($2,495) sit either side of
            it, and the AssaultRunner Classic was listed at $2,799. Eight levels of magnetic resistance matter more than they
            sound: at the top settings the belt becomes a sled push, which turns the machine into a strength tool too. Our <a href="/curved-treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill workouts</a> show
            how to use them.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">$2,995 to $4,600: premium</h3>
          <p>
            The <a href="/assaultrunner-pro-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AssaultRunner Pro</a> at
            $3,499 is the benchmark: Assault&rsquo;s comparison table lists a 7-year frame and 3-year parts warranty and a
            150,000-mile belt. The TrueForm Trainer ($3,495 rubber, $3,795 turf) and Woodway Curve LTG ($3,495) sit at the
            same price. Pay the extra $1,000 for the AssaultRunner Elite ($4,499) mainly for its 10-year frame warranty,
            lifetime belt cover and one year of labour.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">$6,000 and up: top end and club machines</h3>
          <p>
            TrueForm&rsquo;s Runner is configured to order, from $6,195 for the Enduro version on a rubber surface to $9,195
            with a turf, track or Naboso surface and the TrueTouch display. The NOHrD Sprintbok is a solid-wood machine
            from $8,199. Technogym&rsquo;s Skillmill, at $15,250, is a club product. At this level you are paying for a
            particular curve, surface or finish, not for faster or harder training than a $3,500 machine allows. See our <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">TrueForm guide</a> and <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway guide</a> for those ranges. Technogym’s home treadmills start lower, with the <a href="/technogym-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Technogym MyRun</a> at $5,390.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-more" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why a Curved Treadmill Costs More Than a Motorised One</h2>
<p>
            It looks odd that a treadmill with no motor, no incline system and often no power cord costs more than a
            motorised one. Sole sells both, which makes the gap easy to see: on Sole&rsquo;s own store on 23 November 2026
            the C80 curved trainer was $2,099.99, while the motorised F80, with a 10.1-inch touchscreen, a brushless motor
            and incline to 15%, was $1,499.99, and the F63 was $1,099.99. Five things explain the difference.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The slat belt is the expensive part.</strong> A curved treadmill&rsquo;s running surface is dozens of
            individual slats, each riding on bearings along a curved steel track. That is more parts, more machining and
            more assembly than a single fabric belt over a flat deck.</li>
            <li><strong>The frame has to hold a curve.</strong> The deck is a fixed, shaped steel structure rather than a
            flat board, and it is heavy: Assault lists the AssaultRunner at 280 lb assembled and 359.8 lb boxed, and the
            Sole C80 is about 261 lb. Heavy steel costs more to make and to ship.</li>
            <li><strong>They are built to gym standards.</strong> Curved trainers came from commercial gyms and sports
            training, and many home models are close cousins of club machines. Warranties such as Assault&rsquo;s
            150,000-mile belt cover reflect that build.</li>
            <li><strong>Far fewer are made.</strong> Motorised folding treadmills sell in huge numbers; curved trainers are
            a niche. Lower volume means each machine carries more of the design, tooling and shipping cost.</li>
            <li><strong>No folding, no flat-pack savings.</strong> Curved trainers do not fold and usually arrive as one
            heavy piece on a freight pallet, which is costlier than a boxed folding treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What you get back for that premium is a belt with no top speed, instant changes of pace, no motor to burn out
            and, on the better machines, very long belt life. What you give up is set pace, incline and folding. Our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorised treadmill</a> comparison weighs those trade-offs in full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Running Costs: No Electricity, No Subscription</h2>
<p>
            Once a curved treadmill is in place, it costs very little to keep. That is the strongest part of the money
            argument for one.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Electricity: none.</strong> The belt is driven by you. The Sole C80 and AssaultRunner listings and
            SB Fitness&rsquo;s product pages all state that no electricity is needed. Consoles run on batteries or on power
            the belt generates, depending on the model.</li>
            <li><strong>Subscription: none required.</strong> None of the curved treadmills here needs a membership to
            work. Sunny&rsquo;s listings advertise the SunnyFit app with no membership fees, and the Sole C80&rsquo;s Bluetooth
            console connects to apps but does not need one.</li>
            <li><strong>Maintenance: light.</strong> There is no belt to lubricate in the way a motorised deck needs, and
            no motor, motor controller or incline motor to fail. SB Fitness describes its CT400 as maintenance-free, and
            the Sole C80 listing says it needs less maintenance than motorised models. Keep the slats clean and check for
            noise from the bearings.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For comparison, our <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill electricity guide</a> works out about $3 a month for 45 minutes of daily jogging on a motorised machine at the US average of about
            17 cents per kWh, and our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a> puts routine motorised upkeep at $15 to $40 a year. Neither is large; the bigger risk on a motorised machine is a
            motor or board repair out of warranty.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The parts that can cost money</h3>
          <p>
            A curved treadmill is not repair-free, and its parts are specialist. TrueForm&rsquo;s own parts store on 23
            November 2026 listed a replacement Runner KRI belt at $2,295 and a Naboso barefoot belt at $2,675, alongside
            small parts such as a guide roller assembly at $9.89 and a one-way bearing at $154. That is why belt and parts
            warranties matter more on a curved trainer than the headline frame term. Compare Assault&rsquo;s three tiers: 2
            years of parts on the Classic, 3 on the Pro and Elite, and belt cover from none to lifetime. Rogue Fitness, which resells Woodway, TrueForm and STEPR trainers, listed eight treadmills from $3,495 to $11,395 on 5 November 2026, as our <a href="/rogue-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Rogue Fitness treadmill guide</a> shows.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="five-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Curved vs Motorised: The Five-Year Cost</h2>
<p>
            Here is what each setup costs over five years at today&rsquo;s prices, with no repairs out of warranty and no
            price rises assumed. The motorised machines include about $180 of electricity (the $3 a month figure above, for
            60 months) and $75 to $200 of routine upkeep ($15 to $40 a year).
          </p>
