              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm Carbon Pro 2000 + iFIT Pro</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$255&ndash;$380</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,340</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $3,794&ndash;$3,919</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Sole prices from Sole&rsquo;s store on 23 November 2026; the ProForm price and iFIT Pro at $39 a month are from
            our <a href="/how-much-does-a-proform-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm price guide</a> (Amazon, 22 November 2026). The sums show three things. First, against a subscription-free motorised treadmill
            of the same brand, a curved trainer still costs more over five years: the C80 is about $220 to $345 more than
            the F80 once power and upkeep are counted. Second, it closes most of the gap with a dearer motorised model: the
            C80 comes out slightly below the <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>F85</a>. Third, against a motorised treadmill with a $39-a-month class membership,
            even a $3,499 AssaultRunner Pro is cheaper by year five.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the running-cost savings are real but small. Buy a curved treadmill because you want what it does, not to
            save money on electricity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Curved Treadmill Used</h2>
<p>
            Because a curved trainer has no motor and a long-lived slat belt, a used one can be a better bet than a used
            motorised treadmill, where the motor and control board are the expensive unknowns. The catch is supply: few are
            sold, and many second-hand ones come out of gyms and box studios after heavy use.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Where they turn up:</strong> local listings, gym closures and equipment resellers. Our <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a> guide lists the channels.</li>
            <li><strong>Factory seconds and scratch-and-dent:</strong> SB Fitness has sold scratch-and-dent CT550 and CT700
            units below its normal prices (we saw $1,745 and $2,245 on 14 October 2026), and Woodway sells certified
            pre-owned treadmills on its own store. A maker-backed second usually keeps some warranty, which a private sale
            does not.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty does not follow the machine:</strong> most home warranties cover the original buyer only,
            so price a used curved trainer as if it has none.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to check on a used curved treadmill</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Walk and then run on it. The belt should start, speed up and stop smoothly, with no grinding, clicking or
            slats catching.</li>
            <li>Look along the slats for cracks, missing rubber or uneven gaps.</li>
            <li>Ask about the resistance on models that have it: every level should feel different.</li>
            <li>Check the console works, and how it is powered (batteries, or a charger that may be missing).</li>
            <li>Plan the move. At 260 to 280 lb or more, a curved trainer needs two or three strong people and a dolly.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A sensible ceiling: if a used machine is within a few hundred dollars of a new SB Fitness CT400 or Sole C80
            with a warranty, buy new. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the rest of the inspection, and <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used</a> weighs the trade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Installation and Other Costs</h2>
<p>
            The sticker price is most of the cost, but not all of it. Budget for these before you compare models.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Delivery:</strong> curved trainers arrive by freight. Tru Grit&rsquo;s Amazon listing warns that the
            Runner requires delivery by appointment, and the AssaultRunner ships in a 359.8 lb box, per Assault. Check
            whether delivery is to the kerb, the garage or the room.</li>
            <li><strong>Installation:</strong> TrueForm sells white-glove installation as a separate item at $899 on its
            store (23 November 2026). Elsewhere, ask whether the machine arrives assembled; the Rongle listing says no
            assembly is required.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns:</strong> a restocking fee on a heavy machine can be large. On a $1,995 trainer, a 20% fee is
            about $400, as our CT550 review notes, plus return freight. Try a curved treadmill at a gym first if you can.</li>
            <li><strong>Flooring:</strong> a rubber mat under a 260 lb-plus machine protects the floor; see our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats</a> guide.</li>
            <li><strong>Heart-rate strap:</strong> some consoles read a chest strap that is sold separately, such as the
            HR200 that Sunny&rsquo;s listing mentions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a> explains the delivery options and what to ask before you book.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-when" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where and When Curved Treadmills Are Cheapest</h2>
<p>
            Curved trainer prices vary more between sellers than most treadmills, so check more than one place.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Direct can beat Amazon.</strong> The SB Fitness CT400 was $1,395 on SB Fitness&rsquo;s store but
            $1,595 on Amazon on 23 November 2026. The Sole C80 was the same $2,099.99 in both places.</li>
            <li><strong>Black Friday sales are live.</strong> Sole&rsquo;s store showed the C80 at $2,099.99 against a
            regular $2,399.99 on 22 and 23 November. Woodway&rsquo;s site advertised a Fall and Black Friday sale through Tuesday 1
            December, with $500 off the Curve and $250 off the Curve LTG. Black Friday itself is 27 November 2026; our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill Black Friday deals</a> page tracks offers.</li>
            <li><strong>&ldquo;Compare at&rdquo; prices are not always real.</strong> SB Fitness shows its CT400 against a
            $2,495 &ldquo;compare at&rdquo; price; judge the deal against what other sellers actually charge, not the
            struck-through figure.</li>
            <li><strong>Stock comes and goes.</strong> All three AssaultRunners showed as sold out on Assault&rsquo;s US
            store on 23 November. If you need one by a date, ask a dealer for a confirmed delivery window.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-pay" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Pay for a Curved Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            A curved treadmill is worth its price for a fairly specific buyer. Pay it if most of these describe you:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You do intervals, sprints or HIIT.</strong> No motor means no waiting for the belt to speed up or
            slow down; you change pace the instant you change effort.</li>
            <li><strong>You want a machine with no top speed.</strong> Fast sprinters and field-sport athletes can outrun
            most home motorised treadmills; a curved belt goes as fast as you do.</li>
            <li><strong>You have a permanent spot for it.</strong> It will not fold, and it weighs over 250 lb on the
            better models.</li>
            <li><strong>You want no subscription and no power cord.</strong> Garages, outbuildings and rooms without a
            dedicated circuit all suit a curved trainer.</li>
            <li><strong>You want resistance training too.</strong> The magnetic levels on the C80, CT700 and similar turn the
            belt into a sled push.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Do not pay it if you mainly walk, want to follow a set pace for long easy runs, need incline for hiking
            training, or want to fold the machine away. For those, a motorised incline treadmill does the job better for
            less: compare the Sole F80 at $1,499.99, or our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> for other subscription-free options.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How much should you spend?</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Mostly walking, no power outlet:</strong> an SB Fitness CT250 at $1,095, or a curved walking pad
            under $800 if your budget is fixed.</li>
            <li><strong>Mixed walking, jogging and some intervals:</strong> the SB Fitness CT400 at $1,395 direct.</li>
            <li><strong>Regular sprints and HIIT:</strong> the Sole C80 at $2,099.99 is the value pick, with eight
            resistance levels and a wide deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy daily use or several users:</strong> the AssaultRunner Pro at $3,499, mainly for its warranty
            and belt cover, if you can get one in stock.</li>
            <li><strong>A shallower, more natural curve:</strong> the TrueForm Trainer from $3,495.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="cheap-curved" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Are Cheap Curved Treadmills Worth It?</h2>
<p>
            Amazon is full of curved manual treadmills between $700 and $1,600 from brands with little track record, such
            as the RUNOW at $2,399.99 or unbranded 8-level models at $1,569 to $1,859 on 23 November 2026. Some may be fine;
            the problem is that their listings rarely state the things that decide how a curved trainer lasts: slat
            construction, bearing type, the belt warranty and who handles parts.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are spending $1,500 or more, an SB Fitness, Sunny or Sole model with a named warranty is the safer use of
            the money. RUNOW&rsquo;s listing, for example, offers &ldquo;2 years replacement&rdquo; without defining what it
            covers, against Sunny&rsquo;s stated 3-year structural frame warranty and Sole&rsquo;s lifetime frame. And below
            about $1,000, buy one of the walking pads only for walking: their short rubber tracks are not built for running.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a Curved Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from comparing the wrong machines or the wrong prices.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a curved walking pad to run on</h3>
              <p>Under $1,000, curved machines have short rubber tracks; the JOROTO listing itself says walking and light jogging only. For running, budget from about $1,400.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a marketplace price</h3>
              <p>An Amazon "AssaultRunner" listing showed $5,999.99 while Assault charged $3,499 for the Pro. Check the maker's own price and the seller before paying.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the belt and parts warranty</h3>
              <p>A replacement TrueForm Runner belt was $2,295 on TrueForm's store. Compare belt and parts cover, not just the frame term.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying curved to save on running costs</h3>
              <p>A motorised treadmill costs about $3 a month in power. The saving is small; buy curved for sprints and intervals, not for the electricity bill.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A curved treadmill costs about $1,100 to $4,500 for a real slatted trainer in November
            2026</strong>, with the Sole C80 at $2,099.99 and the AssaultRunner Pro at $3,499 the reference points. Below
            $1,000 you get a curved walking pad; above $6,000, a TrueForm Runner, Sprintbok or club machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is no electricity or subscription to pay, but that saves only a few hundred dollars over five years, so buy
            one for sprints, intervals and HIIT, not to cut running costs. Our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best curved treadmills</a> shortlist the models worth the money.
          </p>
        </div>

