        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a curved treadmill cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A real slatted curved treadmill costs about $1,100 to $4,500, and most home buyers pay $1,900 to $3,500. On 23 November 2026 the SB Fitness CT400 was $1,395 direct, the Sole C80 $2,099.99 and the AssaultRunner Pro $3,499. Top-end models such as the TrueForm Runner run $6,195 to $9,195.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the cheapest curved treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The cheapest curved machines on Amazon were walking pads from $679.99 (JOROTO) on 23 November 2026, but they are for walking and light jogging. The cheapest slatted trainer we found was the SB Fitness CT250 at $1,095 direct, and for running the CT400 at $1,395 direct.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why are curved treadmills so expensive?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The slatted belt is many separate slats running on bearings along a shaped steel track, which costs more to make than a fabric belt on a flat deck. The frames are heavy (about 260 to 280 lb on the Sole C80 and AssaultRunner), built to gym standards, made in small numbers and shipped by freight.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is an AssaultRunner?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On Assault Fitness's US store on 23 November 2026 the AssaultRunner Classic was $2,799, the Pro $3,499 and the Elite $4,499, all shown as sold out. Be wary of marketplace listings far above or below those prices.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do curved treadmills use electricity?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The belt is driven by your stride, and the Sole C80, AssaultRunner and SB Fitness listings all state that no electricity is needed. Consoles run on batteries or on power the belt generates, depending on the model.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a curved treadmill worth the money?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For sprints, intervals and HIIT, and for a garage with no power, yes. For walking, long steady runs or incline training, a motorised treadmill such as the $1,499.99 Sole F80 does more for less.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a curved treadmill cheaper to run than a motorised one?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Slightly. It has no electricity cost, no required subscription and little routine upkeep, but a motorised treadmill only costs about $3 a month in power at average US rates. Over five years the saving is a few hundred dollars, unless the motorised machine needs a paid class membership.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

